A sort holds page locks that nothing can release, and this covers the fix.

A non-SI reader's page locks are released from cursor_move_postop()
(db/sqlglue.c), which runs once per BtCursor move.  A sort has no cursor moves
in it: OP_SorterSort is a single VDBE instruction that calls
sqlite3VdbeSorterRewind(), and for an in-memory sort the entire quicksort
happens inside that one call.  sql_tick() cannot help either, because both of
its call sites are themselves cursor-move paths.  So for as long as the sort
runs, nothing in the process is able to drop this session's page locks, and a
writer that wants one of those pages waits out the whole sort.  On a 1M-row
sort that was measured at 29.5s, with the writer blocked for 29.47s of it in a
single lock wait.

Whether a page lock survives into the sort at all depends on the plan.  comdb2
builds with -DSQLITE_ENABLE_EARLY_CURSOR_CLOSE, so sqlite3WhereEnd() normally
closes each participating cursor as soon as the input scan ends, and comdb2's
sqlite3BtreeCloseCursor() genuinely drops its page lock there.  That close is
skipped for a few plan shapes, MULTI-INDEX OR among them.  In the bytecode for
this test's query the data cursor is closed before the sort but the index
cursor is not, and it is still parked at the tail of the branch it scanned
last -- which is why the writer below deletes a row from the tail of the ixb
branch rather than an arbitrary one.

The fix, sort_release_check_interval, checks for a page-lock waiter from inside
the sorter's merge loop and does a page-locks-only release there.  The curtran
is kept, so table read locks survive and a schema change still cannot slip into
the window.  The check has to be inside the loop rather than once before it,
for two reasons: the waiter that matters usually arrives *during* the sort, so
a check made beforehand would see nothing, and a sort nested in a loop (a
correlated subquery re-runs its sort once per outer row) must not pay a release
per iteration when nobody is waiting.  Releasing mid-merge is safe because the
merge walks in-memory records and touches no btree, and because the read phase
is already over -- every matched row is in the sorter before the sort starts.

Getting a measurable window needed some care.

The writer has to arrive inside the sort, not before or after it.  A sleep
timed against the query start does not work: the scan ahead of the sort varies
with load, so the writer lands in the scan (where releases already happen) or
after the sort has finished.  Instead the server publishes two counters,
sorter_rewinds_started and sorter_rewinds_finished; the difference is how many
sorts are inside the blocking window right now.  The writer polls that and
fires the moment the window opens.

The sort itself has to be slow enough to see.  The lever is not row count: a
plain int sort runs about 0.36us/row, so even 8M matched rows only reach ~3.6s,
and going further does not work because sqlsortermem is an int, capping the
in-memory sort near 2.1GB, past which the sorter spills to a different path.
Nor is it wide keys -- for a fixed sorter budget what matters is comparison
cost per byte of record, and string or blob bytes compare at memory bandwidth.
Measured at 500k matched rows: a plain int key sorts in 0.18s, a 200-char
string key in 0.26s, a 1MB blob key in 0.47s, but 24 tying int columns take
2.90s and 96 take 13.81s.  Columns that tie force sqlite3VdbeRecordCompare() to
decode and compare every field on every comparison, they scale linearly past
24, and they cost almost nothing to load.  They must come first in the ORDER BY
and must actually tie: any discriminating column ahead of them short-circuits
the comparison before it reaches them.

Finally, the reported sort duration comes from the server, via
debug_trace_sorter_rewind.  Query wall time cannot stand in for it: with ORDER
BY no row is returned until the sort completes, so an end-to-end timing
confounds the sort with the pipelining the query gives up.  Attempts to infer
it by differencing against a baseline query produced negative sort times.

The test runs four arms, alternating the fix off and on twice.  With the check
disabled the writer is expected to wait out most of the sort; with it enabled,
to get through in well under a second.  Observed: 7.7s and 7.2s blocked against
sorts of 7746ms and 7185ms, versus 0.04s twice.

It also guards its own premise, and fails rather than passing vacuously if the
plan stops being a MULTI-INDEX OR, if the ORDER BY no longer needs a sort (an
index whose leading columns match it would remove the sorter entirely), if the
sorter spills to disk, or if the sort comes out too short to be a meaningful
window.

Knobs: SORT_ROWS, SORT_TIECOLS, SORT_CHUNK, SORT_MIN_MS, SORT_LATCH_TIMEOUT.
